 ## Overview
-Non-local approximated gradients are a technique for differentiable rendering that approximates the gradient of the rendering integrand with respect to geometric parameters (such as vertex positions) by incorporating gradients backpropagated from a loss function [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard differentiable rendering methods that rely on local geometric derivatives, this approach aims to provide non-zero, directed gradients that guide optimization away from local minima by leveraging the specific optimization objective (e.g., image reconstruction) [S1, unofficial].
